{
	title: 'Web login by one-time code',
	description: "The web password is gone; a browser logs in with a one-time code (questions.md items 26-27). The host issues a code on request: 6 lowercase Crockford base32 characters (30 bits), valid for 10 minutes and for one login, kept in memory only. Codes are shown in exactly two places: the reply to /auth in any tab, and `./run auth` from a shell in the home (it asks the running host, or says none is running). They never appear in the transcript's saved history, a status line or a visible link text, so a screenshot or video leaks nothing that lasts: a terminal link to the web page (OSC 8) carries a fresh code only in its hidden target (?auth=<code>). A browser without a valid session sees one gate: 'Enter a one-time code: type /auth in Hal, or ./run auth in a shell.' A right code, typed or from ?auth=, is swapped for a session token: 20 Crockford characters (100 bits) from crypto.getRandomValues, in an HttpOnly, Secure (except on localhost), SameSite=Strict cookie valid for 30 days; the host stores only a hash of each token, and the page drops ?auth= from the address bar. Wrong codes are rate-limited host-wide (at most 10 a minute), so guessing a 30-bit code inside its 10 minutes is hopeless. /ws needs the cookie and a matching Origin. `/auth revoke` ends every web session. Tests: code expiry and single use, the rate limit, the cookie flow, a stale or revoked token refused, and no code in any history file.",
	status: 'planned',
	needs: ['p6'],
	foldInto: ['p6']
}
